{"case": "conversation", "first_pulse": 2, "final_pulse": 2, "actions": 0, "reply": "A simple naming convention you can use is the **YYYYMMDD\u2011Project\u2011Component** format (e.g., `20231015-Helix-Documentation`). This approach has several advantages:\n\n1. **Chronological Order** \u2013 The YYYYMMDD prefix makes it easy to sort files chronologically, so you always know what was created first without relying on titles.\n2. **Clarity of Scope** \u2013 Adding a Project label (Helix in this case) lets anyone quickly identify the broader context or system the file belongs to, reducing ambiguity when multiple projects are stored together.\n3. **Granularity for Components** \u2013 Including a Component tag distinguishes different types of documentation (e.g., `Design`, `Usage`, `API`) without needing long descriptive titles, making it easier to locate specific sections later.\n4. **Scalability** \u2013 This pattern works whether you\u2019re documenting code, design specs, or user guides, and can be extended with additional prefixes if needed (e.g., `20231015-Helix-Code\u2011Module`).\n\nYou could also consider appending a version number at the end (e.g., `-v1`) if updates are expected, but for most static documentation this three-part format is sufficient. It keeps filenames short, predictable, and self\u2011explanatory while minimizing friction when you need to locate or sort files quickly."}
